  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550880,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "likely to appease the Senate Banking Committee \nto get worse confirmed and in place as quickly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as possible. Powell has, however, expressed \nhis intention to remain on the board anyway,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least until the matter is fully resolved. \nAnother potential motivating factor for his hold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out is also to run interference for his colleague \nLisa Cook, another Fed governor who is also under  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investigation, also as thinly veiled political \npressure to get the board to drop interest rates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or to make way for someone who will. So again, \nironically, a political move that was intended  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to speed up Powell's departure and bring on those \nsweet, sweet interest rate cuts may ironically end  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up making both take much longer. Powell sticking \naround could also do more damage to this objective  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than just losing an extra friendly vote on the \nboard, too. It is entirely possible that if Powell  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stays on as just a regular governor until the end \nof his term in 2028, he could end up holding more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599680,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actual sway over the board than whoever the \nofficial chairman ends up being. And that is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assuming they get through the Senate in the first \nplace. Powell is the person the other governors  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are used to working with. He knows the building. \nHe knows the staff. He knows the data teams. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613040,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a collegial committee, a respected ex-chair \nwith a vote in a decade of institutional memory is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not nothing. So that is the brief summation of the \ncluster of a power struggle currently happening at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Fed. And all of this would already be very bad \neven if the wider economy was otherwise stable.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is kind of like a ship going through a \nmutiny right as it is sailing into a storm. So  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634880,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's time to learn how money works to find out \nif the Fed is still going to be a stabilizing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force right when we need it the most. This video \nis sponsored by Monarch Money. Monarch Money is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a private ad-free personal finance platform that \nbrings all your accounts into one clean dashboard.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649760,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It automatically tracks your spending and net \nworth and helps you stay financially aligned  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without juggling spreadsheets. Instead of just \nshowing where your money went, Monarch makes it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easy to see the full financial picture, make smart \nadjustments, and make progress towards your goals  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663440,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with confidence. Plus, with bank level encryption \nand readonly access, it is secure and thousands of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "users have given it a 4.9 star rating. I started \nusing Monarch for my monthly budget check-ins and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it has been a gamecher. All my accounts, cards, \nloans, and investments sync from over 13,000  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681360,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "institutions into one beautiful view on web or \nmobile, saving me time hopping between apps. I  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "love the flexible budgeting that separates fixed \nand flexible spending with customizable rules  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and categories. and Monarch quietly comes through \nyour numbers and surfaces patterns for you. Things  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like spending changes or unusual transactions \nexplained in plain English so you stay aware  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without constant alerts. There is also a built-in \nassistant you can ask anything about your finances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707440,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like how much you spent on travel last summer \nor whether you can afford a vacation without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "touching your savings. It answers from your actual \ndata, not generic advice. Try Monarch yourself.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717680,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Scan the QR code on screen and use code HMW50 \nfor 50% off your first year. Okay, so assuming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726240,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the secession issue actually gets sorted out \nwithin the coming weeks, it's also important  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to understand the economic environment that any \nnew chairman is going to walk into. And it is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to put it generously, not ideal. The first and \nmost immediate problem is raw political pressure.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The White House very openly wants interest rates \ndown because lower rates normally gives the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economy a short-term boost at the long-term \ncost of higher inflation. Cheaper borrowing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will also usually drive up asset markets, which \nmeans wealthy investors like Wors and a lot of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the senior government officials currently pushing \nfor rate cuts stand to directly profit from looser  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monetary policy. The obvious problem, putting \naside the personal profits over good governance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conversation, is that asset markets are already \npretty damn juiced at the moment. While at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same time, most regular people are measurably \nstruggling with the cost of living that would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only be made worse by further inflation. Asset \nmarkets are near an all-time high, while consumer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sentiment is near an all-time low, which is not a \ngreat sign for statistics that in a normal economy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be highly correlated. In the past, the \nstock market did well when the businesses listed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792160,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on that market did well, and the businesses \nlisted on that market did well when consumers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796640,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were consuming lots of stuff. And consumers only \nconsume lots of stuff when they have confidence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they won't lose their job or be hit with \nrising costs in every direction. Logically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it makes sense, but according to the data, it's \njust not the world we live in at the moment. Which  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brings us to problem number two. The data the Fed \nis actually looking at might not even be real.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The government's statistics around inflation and \nemployment have become increasingly questionable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over the last few years. The payroll report for \nthe last month said America added 178,000 jobs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the labor force in the exact same report \nshrank by 396,000. Inflation measures have been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quietly reweed more than once. And that's not \nto mention the other issues that I have spent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "three out of the last seven videos on this channel \nranting about because they seem so detached from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the economy the rest of us appear to be living in, \nwhich is not a great place for a central bank to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be because the entire triple mandate of the Fed is \nwrapped up in these numbers. If the data is wrong,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854960,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there is not much stopping the decisions from \nbeing wrong. And if the data is wrong in a way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859280,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is politically convenient, the incentive \nto fix it gets even weaker. None of this would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be a crisis on its own. But the third problem \nis that there are real identifiable supply side  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forces pushing prices up and they are almost \nall things the Fed has zero ability to actually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874480,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fix. Tariffs have been layered into almost \nevery major supply chain, at least in theory,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879280,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if their actual implementation remains \ngenuinely up in the air from one week to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "next. Some outside analysts have suggested that \ninflation could have been more or less completely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solved by now if it were not for tariffs \nkeeping import prices elevated on uncertainty.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was also a sticking point for Worsh during \nhis Senate questioning because he was forced to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897360,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "walk the line between appeasing the administration \nthat was appointing him and the economic reality  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he would actually have to face once in the \njob. Because cutting rates into a tariff-driven  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905520,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflation shock is kind of the central banker's \nequivalent of pouring napom on a kitchen fire.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is before you get to the fourth problem \nwhich is the war in Iran. Everything downstream  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of oil is getting more expensive. And that \nis basically everything. Shipping, energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "food, fertilizer, plastic parts, even sausage \nraincoats are going to get more expensive. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the market has more or less priced in \na short temporary spike, a prolonged oil war  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would drive supplyside inflation into an already \ninflated economy. Now, to play devil's advocate,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there is one variable that the nominated incoming \nFed share has highlighted as a reason why it would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be reasonable to start cutting rates. And he kind \nof has a point, although perhaps not necessarily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the reasons he expects. Productivity \ngains from AI could in theory lower the cost  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of providing a lot of services and pass those \nsavings through into lower consumer prices. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 956560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this has been a popular narrative amongst groups \npushing for lower rates, including Worsh himself.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In practice though, at least for now, any real \nproductivity gains from AI are currently being  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more than entirely offset by capital spending and \nenergy costs going to fuel its development. And  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that is assuming that any downstream productivity \nadvantages get passed along to consumers. However,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that doesn't mean that this technology won't \njustify a rate cut because inflation is just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one of the three mandates that the Fed has \nto compromise between. AI is also fueling or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the very least justifying widespread layoffs \nand layoffs reduce employment, which is another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "variable that the Fed is tasked with controlling. \nSo, this may be a point in favor of cutting those  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998160,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rates. And we know this is not speculation because \nmost of these companies have been more or less  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003040,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "openly bragging about it on their quarterly \nearnings call as the main reason their margins  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are holding up. So even the one deflationary \nvariable we have is mostly being pointed at wage  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suppression rather than price suppression. Which \nbrings us to the fifth and by far the biggest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem and it is the one nobody at the Fed really \nwants to say out loud. Right now there is still  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot of faith in the US dollar as the global \nreserve asset. But that faith is not infinite and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are already signs it is cracking. If the Fed \ncuts rates in spite of visible inflation, it risks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerating an already existing global shift away \nfrom the US dollar as a reserve holding. And this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has the potential to rapidly compound in itself. \nA weaker dollar means imports get more expensive,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044320,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means domestic inflation gets worse. A \nweaker appetite for US debt means the Treasury  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has to pay higher yields to find buyers, which \nmakes borrowing more expensive for literally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everyone downstream. Corporate debt is priced off \ntreasuries. Mortgages are priced off treasuries.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060320,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Car loans, student loans, small business loans, \nall priced off treasuries. So, the scenario where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Fed cuts the short end of the curve and \nlong-term rates go up anyway because the bond  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "market is scared is a real thing that can actually \nhappen. Despite no official rate changes from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075120,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fed at all, the US government's cost of borrowing \nthrough the 10-year Treasury is already up around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "40 basis points since the start of the war. On \na debt stack this large, 40 basis points works  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085200,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out to tens of billions of dollars in additional \ninterest payments, which is real money, even by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "federal budget standards. And that is the market \nrepricing US debt before the Fed has even moved.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, if investors lose faith in this institution, \nrate cuts could ironically send long-term rates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even higher, as bond buyers demand a bigger risk \npremium for holding our money. Now, none of these  "
    } ]
  }, {
